## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates expertise in establishing end-to-end provenance for runtime plugins in multi-tenant CNF gateway environments, ensuring secure plugin lifecycle management with industry-standard tools.

## Key Concepts
- Cosign image signing with KMS-backed cryptographic keys
- SPDX SBOM generation and attachment to plugin images
- Per-tenant plugin policy enforcement via ConfigMap allowlists
- Admission webhook validation for signed plugin loading
- Gateway status API integration for provenance visibility
- Namespace-scoped validation with minimal deployment footprint

## Code Example
```go
// Admission webhook validating signed plugin images per tenant
package main

func validatePluginImage(imageRef, tenantNamespace string) error {
    // Verify Cosign signature using KMS public key
    if !cosign.Verify(imageRef, kmsPublicKey) {
        return fmt.Errorf("unsigned plugin image: %s", imageRef)
    }

    // Validate SBOM presence and integrity
    if !sbom.Validate(imageRef) {
        return fmt.Errorf("missing or invalid SBOM for: %s", imageRef)
    }

    // Check against tenant-specific allowlist
    if !allowlist.Contains(tenantNamespace, imageRef) {
        return fmt.Errorf("plugin not in tenant allowlist: %s", imageRef)
    }
    return nil
}
```

## Follow-up Considerations
- Implement automated key rotation for long-term security
- Design plugin update workflows maintaining provenance continuity
- Configure monitoring for plugin compliance violations across tenants

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design per-tenant runtime feature controls using a Redis-backed flag store with TTL-based caching. It evaluates understanding of multi-tenancy, caching strategies, fallback logic, and operational observability in a CNF gateway context.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant feature flags in a Kubernetes sidecar architecture
- Redis key design using tenantID:path format
- TTL-based caching with 60-second expiration
- Fallback mechanism to global default flags
- Runtime observability via /flags/status endpoint

## Code Example

```javascript
// Feature flag evaluation in CNF sidecar
class FeatureFlagService {
  constructor(redisClient) {
    this.redis = redisClient;
    this.globalDefault = true;
  }

  async evaluateFlag(tenantId, path) {
    const key = `${tenantId}:${path}`;
    const cached = await this.redis.get(key);

    if (cached !== null) {
      return cached === 'true';
    }

    return this.globalDefault;
  }

  async getStatus(tenantId) {
    const flags = await this.redis.keys(`${tenantId}:*`);
    const activeFlags = await Promise.all(
      flags.map(async (key) => ({
        path: key.split(':')[1],
        enabled: (await this.redis.get(key)) === 'true',
        ttl: await this.redis.ttl(key)
      }))
    );

    return {
      tenantId,
      flags: activeFlags,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
    };
  }
}
```

## Redis Seed Configuration

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: ConfigMap
metadata:
  name: redis-seed
  namespace: cnf-system
data:
  seed.sh: |
    #!/bin/bash
    redis-cli SET "tenant-a:/api/v1/features" "true" EX 60
    redis-cli SET "tenant-a:/api/v2/features" "false" EX 60
    redis-cli SET "tenant-b:/api/v1/features" "true" EX 60
```

